SEGMENTS|33|14|lines|text|1.0|5.0|A payment gateway keeps its receipts in a ledger it audits every night. When a charge fails twice in a row the gateway parks it, and a person reads the parked n…|||A payment gateway keeps its receipts in a ledger it audits every night. When a charge fails twice in a row the gateway parks it, and a person reads the parked notes in the morning. Most of them are cards that expired mid-trip; a few are banks that went away for the night and came back friendly.

The ledger is small on purpose. A clerk can read a week of it in an afternoon, and the clerk notices what a machine misses: the customer who calls from a landline, the invoice printed on a ship, the refund that should have been a discount.

Nobody indexes the ledger for punishment. The index exists so that when a customer reads a line from an old letter aloud, somebody can find the letter, the charge, and the night the bank went away.|~
SEGMENTS|34|11|lines|text|1.0|7.0|The relay keeps the engine's numbers as they are. A run that stops mid-file keeps the pages it read; the next pass goes on from there. Estimates come from what …|||# Release notes

The relay keeps the engine's numbers as they are. A run that stops mid-file keeps the pages it read; the next pass goes on from there. Estimates come from what indexing measured on this machine, not from a table of hopes.

## Known costs

A photo costs one vector. A scanned page costs a render and a vector. A book costs a first step of thirty pages and the patience of the pass that finishes it.|~
